#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fea5b8c6-a5f8-500c-8d30-16eca2e9eb27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283433} {:answer/id #uuid "264b9de0-a856-5d1f-95ee-c7b1da5211a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283434} {:answer/id #uuid "ec00a9aa-8e7f-5810-92ef-3fcce823f6f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283435} {:answer/id #uuid "5c25e334-8057-50c8-8011-2f53b901cdac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283436} {:answer/id #uuid "a73ded5d-8e8e-5108-82f0-126c6d521d75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fea5b8c6-a5f8-500c-8d30-16eca2e9eb27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283433} :answer-field/id #uuid "73d98ef6-8da1-5c53-b2ec-615955e2939f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "565e8de7-df66-55ac-a66d-c0a62e0a4213" :question/math-academy-id "q-120478" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{2}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{5}{8}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equations II and III have no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9595ccf2-bd46-546f-b931-e9492dec12b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283440} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0dd378-e426-5ece-88c6-835931a99b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283441} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a01b8d-11af-530b-aff1-e486d1ce535d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283442} {:answer/id #uuid "440db562-7037-5d4d-859e-86ac94f945d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283443} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb7d5ce-6c38-568b-8741-bc109e3512d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3a01b8d-11af-530b-aff1-e486d1ce535d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283442} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd54b657-6ba7-5e6e-926a-1c13f9b55764" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a9e6cde4-30d8-5716-a4a4-97a88908653f" :question/math-academy-id "q-120485" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=7$ $$\n- $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ $$\n- $7\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-5$" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=7$ $$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{7}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e63c770d-eec4-542d-b8bf-70bf303fea49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e63c770d-eec4-542d-b8bf-70bf303fea49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283447} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bafc52d-86ad-5924-a360-b01de3288548" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283446} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8875305e-078e-552b-a05d-aea138e7c7bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8875305e-078e-552b-a05d-aea138e7c7bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283449} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab84f765-dc94-59d1-b280-363f8f0521ae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1d8b5708-f9a1-52b7-84fb-a31b5489b79a" :question/math-academy-id "q-274858" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-3\\sqrt{2}=\\sqrt{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-3\\sqrt{2} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}+3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}})≈1.209\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈1.209.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈1.209.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/assets/2c58ae6d53b409e4fac7c4fb5c4d96b08585b1c2ee8a4f1048f4461026d748af.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈2\\pi -1.209 \\\\  & ≈5.074\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=1.2$ and $x=5.1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28062dbf-815b-5221-ae86-1050e106c5ea" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33429208-3bc3-5ef0-a178-d1e0245532bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33429208-3bc3-5ef0-a178-d1e0245532bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283452} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b3d7ade-b9c3-5446-819c-480371181f33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283451} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "424e8bb0-0093-57b7-805e-e45886645e19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "424e8bb0-0093-57b7-805e-e45886645e19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283454} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc5599ac-f9d9-5c53-a2d7-4a83f2ab2bb8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ebd67a4b-f6d9-505f-a725-3907b72f6b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-325107" :question/problem "Find the **exact** solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-2\\pi <x<0.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arccos\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is not in the domain $-2\\pi <x<0.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/assets/17803dc8d1d3ca2e31586969797b401d5d0a2d958bd6628387b512974076df7a.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the fourth quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $X=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ is not in the domain $-2\\pi <x<0.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ and $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Secant with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f30b2415-c7f3-5638-aebb-394e6faf2d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,013" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283457} {:answer/id #uuid "71848f58-8d83-59d3-9dfa-6b0428f8963c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,805" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283458} {:answer/id #uuid "325b8fb5-5ea6-51e1-b06e-42eb7bbc1b37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283459} {:answer/id #uuid "aae87b4f-4922-5fa5-8bf2-22671510849a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21\\,642" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283460} {:answer/id #uuid "1db1b7d6-83e2-5e8d-ac07-73b64857230d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,681" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f30b2415-c7f3-5638-aebb-394e6faf2d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,013" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283457} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea957e06-e2e6-59af-8dd6-d30a4ff6cca1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21067b43-552c-57fe-bdc4-8f094139d29a" :question/math-academy-id "q-76493" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{7}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{2}{7})=106.601\\,549{…}^{∘}≈{106.602}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${106.602}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={106.602}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={73.398}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={73.398}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989949/assets/e378560486eca17f870b21743718c9f984f2bd3bf5609601f1cea674e15018e6.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{73.398}^{∘} \\\\  & ={253.398}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=106.602\\cdot 253.398≈27\\,013,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}]}
